{"records":[{"id":"6k6ng65623","title":"Generation of host-directed and virus-specific antivirals using targeted protein degradation promoted by small molecules and viral RNA mimics","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD), as exemplified by proteolysis-targeting chimera (PROTAC) is an emerging drug discovery platform. PROTAC molecules, which typically contain a target protein ligand linked to an E3 ligase ligand, recruit a target protein to the E3 ligase to induce its ubiquitination and degradation. Here, we applied PROTAC approaches to develop broad-spectrum antivirals targeting key host factors for many viruses and virus-specific antivirals targeting unique viral proteins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-30T20:02:36.523Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6k6ng65623","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6k6ng65623"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Ho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanye","last_name":"Meng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan ","last_name":"Marazzi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Antiviral"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"988sm4nsdx","title":"Ferroptosis Inhibition by Lysosome-Dependent Catabolism of Extracellular Protein","description":"This dataset contains original uncropped western blots, an ImageJ macro, and an R program, related to this work whose abstract is as follows:\n\nCancer cells must ensure a steady supply of nutrients to enable proliferation and evade cell death. Depriving cancer cells of the amino acid cystine can trigger the non-apoptotic cell death process of ferroptosis. Here, we report that cancer cells can evade cystine deprivation-induced ferroptosis by the uptake and catabolism of the cysteine-rich extracellular protein albumin. This protective mechanism is enhanced by mTORC1 inhibition and involves albumin degradation in the lysosome, predominantly by cathepsin B (CTSB). CTSB-dependent albumin breakdown followed by export of cystine from the lysosome via the transporter cystinosin fuels the synthesis of glutathione, which suppresses lethal lipid peroxidation. Notably, when cancer cells are grown under non-adherent conditions as spheroids mTORC1 pathway activity is reduced and albumin supplementation alone affords considerable protection against ferroptosis. These results identify the catabolism of extracellular protein within the lysosome as a mechanism that can inhibit ferroptosis in cancer cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-23T23:12:31.577Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/988sm4nsdx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/988sm4nsdx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Dixon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Cell Death","Lysosomes","Albumin","Necrosis","Proteases"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zkgmjzrcyk","title":"High definition analysis of host protein stability during human cytomegalovirus infection reveals antiviral factors and viral evasion mechanisms, Nightingale et al.","description":"Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is an important pathogen with multiple immune evasion strategies, including virally facilitated degradation of host antiviral restriction factors. Here, we describe a multiplexed approach to discover proteins with innate immune function on the basis of active degradation by the proteasome or lysosome during early phase HCMV infection. Using three orthogonal proteomic/transcriptomic screens to quantify protein degradation, with high confidence we identified 35 proteins enriched in antiviral restriction factors. A final screen employed a comprehensive panel of viral mutants to predict viral genes that target >250 human proteins. This approach revealed Helicase-like Transcription Factor (HLTF), a DNA helicase important in DNA repair, potently inhibits early viral gene expression but is rapidly degraded during infection. The functionally unknown HCMV protein UL145 facilitates HLTF degradation by recruiting the Cullin4 E3 ligase complex. Our approach and data will enable further identifications of innate pathways targeted by HCMV and other viruses.\n\nSee https://www.cell.com/cell-host-microbe/fulltext/S1931-3128(18)30381-0    for full publication.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-19T19:48:42.342Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zkgmjzrcyk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zkgmjzrcyk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Virology","Proteomics","Human Cytomegalovirus"],"journal_issn":["19313128"]},{"id":"rvjz3wbz9m","title":"Human cytomegalovirus degrades DMXL1 to inhibit autophagy, lysosomal acidification and viral assembly","description":"Human cytomegalovirus (HCMV) is an important human pathogen and a master regulator of host intrinsic, innate, and adaptive immunity. HCMV hijacks host intracellular compartments to assemble new virions, and lysosomes are essential for this process. We combined a comprehensive proteomic analysis of host proteins targeted for degradation by HCMV with a database of proteins involved in vacuolar acidification. Dmx-like protein 1 (DMXL1) was the only protein that acidifies vacuoles yet is degraded by HCMV.\nSystematic comparison of viral deletion mutants revealed that the uncharacterised 7kDa US33A protein is necessary and sufficient for DMXL1 degradation. Functional experiments using cells stably expressing US33A, or infected with adenovirus vectors expressing US33A, or infected with recombinant HCMV deleted for US33A, demonstrated that HCMV degrades DMXL1 to inhibit lysosomal acidification and autophagic cargo degradation. Formation of the viral assembly compartment, which is known to require lysosomes, occurred significantly later in cells infected with US33A-expressing virus, with reduced viral replication. These data thus identify an entirely new viral strategy for cellular remodelling. US33A recruits the E3 ubiquitin ligase Kip1 ubiquitination-promoting complex (KPC) and acts akin to a proteolysis-targeting chimera (PROTAC) to degrade DMXL1. The potential thus exists to employ US33A in novel therapies for viral infection or rheumatic conditions, in which inhibition of lysosome acidification can attenuate disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-18T15:48:22.446Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rvjz3wbz9m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rvjz3wbz9m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Weekes","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"MR/X000516/1"}],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pzv2st58r6","title":"Original gels and images from \"Synergistic degradation of circular RNAs by RNAseK and lysosome\"","description":"Original blots and fluorescence images supporting \"Synergistic degradation of circular RNAs by RNAseK and lysosome\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-02T11:30:28.707Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pzv2st58r6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pzv2st58r6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuqi","last_name":"Deng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Western Blot","Agarose Gel Electrophoresis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3zygrwcj4t","title":"The contribution of native protein complexes to targeted protein degradation","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD) destroys proteins of interest (POIs) by hijacking the cellular proteolytic machinery. Most proteins in cells exist and function as part of multi-protein or macromolecular complexes, thereby allowing a single protein to control multiple biological processes. Therefore, when a small molecule degrader induces proximity between an E3 ligase and the POI, the macromolecular context of the POI potentially influences the degradation outcomes of the POI and of the complex components. Here, we explore degradation of the eight CK1α-SACK1 (formerly known as FAM83A-H) complexes initiated by molecular glue degraders primarily designed to target Ser/Thr kinase CK1α. We demonstrate that lenalidomide-derived degraders DEG-77 and SJ3149, which selectively target the CK1α isoform, co-degrade multiple SACK1 proteins. We show that the degradation of SACK1 proteins by DEG-77 and SJ3149 requires CK1α, the CUL4ACRBN E3 ligase complex and the proteasome. In cells derived from palmoplantar keratoderma patients harbouring the CK1α-binding deficient SACK1GR265P mutation, DEG-77 targets CK1α and mitotic SACK1D but not SACK1GR265P, highlighting the requirement for CK1α-SACK1 interaction to achieve co-degradation. Our study underscores the importance of POI context in TPD and reinforces the potential for selectively targeting specific protein complexes for degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-29T13:52:59.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3zygrwcj4t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3zygrwcj4t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorraine","last_name":"Glennie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicole","last_name":"Curnutt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gajanan","last_name":"Sathe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brune","last_name":"Le Chatelier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Freya ","last_name":"Goff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin-Feng","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyrell ","last_name":"Cartwright","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karen","last_name":"Dunbar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicola","last_name":"Wood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Macartney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christina ","last_name":"Woo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gopal","last_name":"Sapkota","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Protein Degradation","Chemical Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"knzz9hctcd","title":"Effect of extracellular matrix proteins ","description":"In this in vitro study low concentrations (1, 5 and 10 µg/mL) of fibronectin (FN), laminin (LM) and type I collagen (COL) were used to assess their bioactive effects on human apical papilla cells.\nThe data related to the results obtained through the methodologies developed in this study are organized in spreadsheets (xlsx file).\nThe images related to the evaluation of chemotaxis were grouped in a single file (tif file).\nThe images related to the assessment of cell adhesion and spreading were grouped in a single file (tif file).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-11T18:30:53.236Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/knzz9hctcd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/knzz9hctcd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maria Luisa","last_name":"Leite","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Extracellular Matrix Protein","Dental Pulp"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m62b7d3n8m","title":"Source data Figures 5-6 from \"ATXN3 regulates lysosome regeneration after damage by targeting K48-K63-branched ubiquitin chains\"","description":"ATXN3 regulates lysosome regeneration after damage by targeting K48-K63-branched ubiquitin chains\nMaike Reinders, Bojana Kravic, Pinki Gahlot, Johannes van den Boom, Nina Schulze, Sophie Levantovsky, Stefan Kleine, Markus Kaiser, Yogesh Kulathu, Christian Behrends, Hemmo Meyer\n\nSource data for Figures 5-6 presented in Reinders et al. EMBOJ 2025.\nFor other figures, see respective data sets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-31T16:32:31.067Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m62b7d3n8m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m62b7d3n8m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maike","last_name":"Reinders","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bojana","last_name":"Kravic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pinki","last_name":"Gahlot","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Johannes","last_name":"van den Boom","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nina","last_name":"Schulze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sophie","last_name":"Levantovsky","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stefan","last_name":"Kleine","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Kaiser","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yogesh","last_name":"Kulathu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Behrends","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hemmo","last_name":"Meyer","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"INST 20876/480-1 FUGG"},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"447112704"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"MC_UU_00038/3"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0472cxd90","name":"European Research Council","grant_id":"101002428"},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"INST 20876/294-1 FUGG"},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"424228829"},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"390857198"},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"259130777"}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Lysosome","Membrane","Autophagy","Ubiquitylation","Cellular Stress Response"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yk5fs9v2rj","title":"Sourc